This is my third ascension, and the first character where I really
employed any semblance of ranged combat.  I didn't rely on it past the
early game; I still find it tedious, and prefer the somewhat faster
pace of just bashing the crap out of everything.

Also, after my previous ascension, I came to the conclusion that
nothing I had wished for was anything that I either a) wouldn't have
found elsewhere in the dungeon or b) something I couldn't do without.
I wanted to ascend this Tourist wishless to prove to myself that I
could take the weakest early game character and get him to Astral
without having to rely on the Castle wand.

My first few Tourist games were ugly, mostly defined by not adjusting
to weak starting attributes, and by levelling up faster than I could
find equipment to deal with tough monsters.  I took a few (failed)
stabs at the protection racket.  A particularly promising (general
store with magic marker, and enchant weapon scroll --> +6 long sword
in the very early game) character made the fatal mistake of picking up
a chickatrice corpse so it wouldn't fall into enemy hands.  While not
wearing gloves.

Cue monkeypox...

All he wanted was a relaxing vacation in the Mazes of Menace, to buy
some stuff, see the sights, maybe take a few photos.  Instead, he got
into a bigger adventure than they ever could have imagined...

Early game was straightforward; threw or wielded daggers, let the pet
do the work.  There was a lawful altar on the first floor, a small
general store on the second (credit-cloned and pet-looted), and an
armor shop on level 3 (nothing exciting to be had here).  I acquired a
strange mental acuity from a floating eye corpse.  My first blessed ID
scroll revealed that the gauntlets I had acquired from a random kobold
were gauntlets of dexterity.  I had also been trawling a +3 orcish
dagger around; this was immediately promoted to primary-weapon status
for the first half of the game.  A ring of poison resistance also came
in handy until I got the intrinsic later.

Sokoban was the Amulet.  I got sleep resistance and a +2 shield from a
Woodland-elf and then did the Mines... I found 2(!) magic markers on
the same level down there.  The Minetown temple was cross-aligned;
Izchak only had 5 candles and an oil lamp.  A gray stone on a nearby
level was price-IDed as a luckstone, and so I skipped Mine's End
(until much later).

Right here is where I spent about 12000 turns pushing boulders back
and forth while trying to get crowned.  I had maxed my luck throwing
gems at a unicorn, but it took literally 19 altar prayers to placate
the RNG.  This prayfest also scored me 5 points of divine protection,
the stealth intrinsic, and a 20-30HP boost from golden glows.

Finally equipped with a decent weapon (and several intrinsics I had
lacked until that point), I finally descended below Sokoban.  Found a
third altar on Dlvl8 (cross-aligned), fourth on Dlvl18 (cross-
aligned), a fifth on 18 as well (cross-aligned temple), and a *sixth*
cross-aligned altar just below on Dlvl19.  That same level also housed
a barracks, where a kind captain donated his silver saber to the
cause.  Dlvl19 also held a magic fountain the portal to Fort Ludios;
though I had reflection, I wanted MR as well before venturing in.  I
returned to Dlvl12 to do the quest.

The Tourist quest is easy and has too much stuff.  Important items I
found along the way included a bag of holding (finally), a cursed -1
oilskin cloak, and a good amount of EA/EW scrolls.  A blessed ID
scroll revealed that the rusty helm I'd been wearing around for the
past 25000 turns was a +0 Helm of Brilliance.  The Master of Thieves
was quickly dispatched, and I claimed the Platinum Yendorian Express
Card for my troubles.

By this point, I had found 3 scrolls of genocide, which were blessed
and used to wipe L, h, and ; in that order.

During a quick side trip to blank scrolls and potions, I forgot that
bags of holding are not waterproof.  So much for all my useful scrolls
and potions...

Ludios was fun; I stoned 50+ soldiers on the same square before my
cocaktrice corpse rotted away.  Croesus' throne yielded a genocide
(disenchanters), and the gold from the vault brought me to naked AC:0.

Dlvl28 was Medusa, Dlvl29 was the Castle.  I still lacked a source of
levitation, and speed/jumping boots, but the rest of my kit was pretty
much assembled.  I went downstairs and cleared out the VotD, then came
back upstairs and burned a potion of levitation to clear out the
castle storerooms.  Here I made a suit of BDSM to replace the +0
dwarvish mithril coat that had served as my body armor until that
point.  The castle contained nothing else of use.

Gehennom, the short version: Dlvl33 held boots of levitation; 34 was
Asmodeus (bribed), 35 was Baalzebub (bribed); 36 was Juiblex (dug); 39
was Orcus (stoned; he was carrying *two* wands of death, which were
the only ones I found all game); 42 was the Wizard and the entrance to
Vlad's tower (there were also speed boots lying on the ground, for
which I was quite thankful); I replaced my BDSM with SDSM in Vlad's
and donned his amulet of life saving; and Dlvl49 was the VS level.

I broke polyitemless to polypile for candles.  That totally sucked; I
scoured the dungeon and didn't find any aside from the 5 Izchak had
given me.  Then I figured, if I'm going to polypile these 6 tools, why
not throw 14 scrolls into the mix?  That's how I ended up with 12
blessed scrolls of enchant armor, which translated to an extra 15
points of AC and fooproof gear.

I was decked out and ready to go; the only thing I lacked was a backup
source of magic resistance, but I figured I'd wing it.  Zapped Rodney,
book, levelport, invocation.  "The death ray hits the High Priest of
Moloch." Hmmm... after killing him the hard way, I found a cloak of
magic resistance on his body and donned it ASAP.

The ascension run spanned about 1350 turns and was uneventful; the
wizard showed up three more times in the main dungeon and once on the
plane of earth; and Famine guarded the first altar I visited, which
ironically enough was the only coaligned altar I found all game.


